//
// @EXPECT_ERROR: Cannot redefine 'sum' (already defined at
//
// A name collision with a stdlib free function reports ONCE, and says where the winner is.
//
// `sum` is `pub fn sum <T: Addable>(v: vector<T>, init: T) -> T` in `01_code.loft`, so this
// definition is correctly refused. But the refusal answered with a bare "Cannot redefine
// 'sum'" and then a SECOND error — `Syntax error: unexpected '->'` — against a signature
// that is perfectly well formed: reporting the collision returned `u32::MAX`, which
// `parse_function` reads as "this was not a function", leaving the lexer parked between the
// parameter list and the `->` for the top-level loop to trip over. A reader who trusted the
// second message went looking at their return-type syntax (loft#863).
//
// The guard is as much the absence of the second error as the presence of the first: the
// harness fails on any error it was not told to expect, so a return to the two-message shape
// fails here even though the file still errors.
//
// The sibling branch — a free function shadowed by a METHOD on its first argument's type,
// which `len` takes — always reported once and always named the position. That is the
// behaviour this brings the second branch to, so both now read the same.

fn sum(v: vector<integer>) -> integer {
  t = 0;
  for e in v { t += e; }
  t
}

fn main() {
  println("{sum([1, 2, 3])}");
}
